\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Resolution-controlled paired evaluation. (A) The fixed processed topology $G$ is combined with two separately controlled resolutions: $W$ sampled selection worlds define the finite-world greedy reference, whereas ranking tolerance $\tau $ and coverage $q$ define the restricted candidate pool $C(\tau ,q)$. Full and candidate-restricted greedy use the same graph, world prefix, seed budget and deterministic tie rule; only eligibility differs. (B) The selected sets are compared on 10,000 disjoint evaluation worlds, with ordered trajectory, final-set agreement, paired utility and the stated timing scope reported separately. The diagram describes the evaluation design, not a new influence-maximization algorithm.}}{3}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:evaluation-mechanism}{{1}{3}{Resolution-controlled paired evaluation. (A) The fixed processed topology $G$ is combined with two separately controlled resolutions: $W$ sampled selection worlds define the finite-world greedy reference, whereas ranking tolerance $\tau $ and coverage $q$ define the restricted candidate pool $C(\tau ,q)$. Full and candidate-restricted greedy use the same graph, world prefix, seed budget and deterministic tie rule; only eligibility differs. (B) The selected sets are compared on 10,000 disjoint evaluation worlds, with ordered trajectory, final-set agreement, paired utility and the stated timing scope reported separately. The diagram describes the evaluation design, not a new influence-maximization algorithm}{figure.1}{}}
